  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#newlines\n\n> Newlines in HTML may be represented either as U+000D CARRIAGE RETURN (CR) characters, U+000A LINE FEED (LF) characters, or pairs of U+000D CARRIAGE RETURN (CR), U+000A LINE FEED (LF) characters in that order.\n\nWhy aren't U+2028 LINE SEPARATOR (<i>LS</i>) and U+2029 PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR (<i>PS</i>) included in this list?\nI know that <i>CR</i>+<i>LF</i> and <i>LF</i> are most often used as the <i>NLF</i>, and that HTTP only allows <i>CR</i>+<i>LF</i> as newlines in the protocol, but doesn't the HTML Standard violate Section 5.4 in the Unicode Standard if <i>LS</i> and <i>PS</i> are not allowed as line separators? Or is this for backward-compatibility with systems and frameworks that do not process these code points as newlines?\n",
